papers.abstract:
Let \(0<p,q<\infty\). We prove \[
  L_q(0,1)\hookrightarrow\mathcal C_{p,q}
  \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad q=2. \] As a consequence, we obtain, for all \(0<p_1,q_1,p_2,q_2<\infty\), \[
  L_{p_1,q_1}(0,1)\hookrightarrow\mathcal C_{p_2,q_2}
  \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad p_1=q_1=2. \]

Let $p$ be an odd prime, $a,m\in\Bbb Z_{(p)}$ and $a(a+1)m\not\equiv 0\pmod p$. In this paper, we deduce the congruence for $(\sum_{k=0}^{p-1}\frac{\binom{2k}k\binom ak\binom{-1-a}k}{m^k})
  (\sum_{k=0}^{p-2}\frac{\binom{2k}k\binom ak\binom{-1-a}k}{m^k(k+1)})$ modulo $p^3$.
  As applications we obtain many congruences for \begin{align*}&\sum_{k=0}^{(p-1)/2}\frac{\binom{2k}k^3}
  {m^k(k+1)^r},\quad \sum_{k=0}^{p-2}\frac{\binom{2k}k^2\binom{4k}{2k}}{m^k(k+1)^r}, \quad \sum_{k=0}^{p-2}\frac{\binom{2k}k\binom{3k}k\binom{6k}{3k}} {m^k(k+1)^r}\quad(r=1,2,3) \\&\sum_{k=0}^{(p-1)/2}k^j\frac{\binom{2k}k^3}
  {m^k},\quad \sum_{k=0}^{p-1}k^j\frac{\binom{2k}k^2\binom{4k}{2k}}{m^k}, \quad \sum_{k=0}^{p-1}k^j\frac{\binom{2k}k\binom{3k}k\binom{6k}{3k}} {m^k}\quad(j=2,3) \end{align*} modulo $p^3$, which confirm the author's conjectures and improve previous results modulo $p^2$.

Let $f$ be a transcendental entire solution of order less than 1 of the linear difference equation \begin{equation*}
  \sum_{j=0}^{m}P_j(z)\Delta^j f(z)=0,
  \qquad P_m\not\equiv0, \end{equation*} where $P_0,\ldots,P_m$ are polynomials. Applying the inverse Newton transform $\Scal$, which sends $\fall{z}{n}$ to $z^n$, we obtain a function $g=\Scal f$ satisfying a linear differential equation with polynomial coefficients. We then show that the completely regular growth of $g$ transfers to $f$. Consequently, $f$ is of completely regular growth and the zeros of \(f\) are regularly distributed. An example involving the \(\Gamma\) function is given to shows that the condition order less than $ 1$ cannot be relaxed.

We prove a local higher integrability result for the gradient of \textit{a priori} H\"older continuous weak solutions to the parabolic double phase equation \[ \partial_t u - \operatorname{div} \left(|Du|^{p-2}Du + a(z)|Du|^{q-2}Du\right)
  = 0 \qquad \text{in } \Omega_T. \] We work under a relaxed gap condition on the exponents $p$ and $q$. The coefficient $a$ is assumed to belong to the class $\mathcal{Z}^{\kappa}(\Omega_T)$ for some $\kappa \in (0,\infty)$. The functions in this class satisfy a one-sided pointwise bound that controls how fast $a$ can grow away from its zero set, and the class contains the H\"older continuous functions. We also impose a \emph{mild almost increasing} condition on $a$, which motivates the introduction of a new mollification, which we call the \emph{slanted Steklov average}. For $u \in C^{0,\gamma,\gamma/q}_{\mathrm{loc}}(\Omega_T)$ with $\gamma \in [0,1)$, our main result holds under the gap bound \begin{equation}\tag{G}\label{eq:G}
  2 \le p \le q, \,\,\,\,\,\,\,\,\, (q-p)(q-2\gamma)\leq q\kappa \end{equation} The new gap condition \eqref{eq:G} is purely parabolic in nature and is stricter than the optimal gap relation associated with the Lavrentiev phenomenon for the elliptic double phase functional.
